WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:07.07
This is journey fourteen of Hewn, the software factory: preview product designs.

00:00:07.07 --> 00:00:12.51
Stage two designs every screen of a product in Magic Patterns, in every state,

00:00:12.51 --> 00:00:16.06
and exports the HTML into the project's repository.

00:00:16.06 --> 00:00:21.71
Owners and Viewers want to look at those designs without leaving Mission Control,

00:00:21.71 --> 00:00:26.86
at any size and in either theme, and jump to Magic Patterns to change one.

00:00:26.86 --> 00:00:31.32
This video walks through how it was built: the designs endpoint,

00:00:31.32 --> 00:00:37.11
a preview host on its own origin, the sandboxed frame, the size and theme controls,

00:00:37.11 --> 00:00:38.08
and the tests.

00:00:39.23 --> 00:00:44.56
A user opens a project's Designs and sees a gallery of every screen and state,

00:00:44.56 --> 00:00:48.45
each with a thumbnail and an open in Magic Patterns link.

00:00:48.45 --> 00:00:51.87
Opening a state renders it from a separate origin,

00:00:51.87 --> 00:00:57.20
inside a sandboxed frame that can't read Mission Control's cookies or storage.

00:00:57.20 --> 00:01:01.16
And the user picks X S, S, M, L or X L, and light or dark,

00:01:01.16 --> 00:01:04.57
and the frame resizes and switches theme to match.

00:01:05.72 --> 00:01:06.89
Here is the flow.

00:01:06.89 --> 00:01:09.96
The Designs tab asks the API for the designs.

00:01:09.96 --> 00:01:15.30
The API reads the manifest stage two wrote, at the head of the default branch,

00:01:15.30 --> 00:01:18.10
and signs a preview link for that commit.

00:01:18.10 --> 00:01:21.73
The frame then loads each page from the preview host,

00:01:21.73 --> 00:01:26.72
which checks the signature and serves the file at that commit, sandboxed.

00:01:27.87 --> 00:01:33.16
The designs handler reads docs slash mocks slash manifest dot json from GitHub,

00:01:33.16 --> 00:01:35.23
with the commit it was read at.

00:01:35.23 --> 00:01:39.79
Each screen gives its title, its Magic Patterns link and its states,

00:01:39.79 --> 00:01:42.60
and each state its path and a preview URL.

00:01:42.60 --> 00:01:46.95
A project before stage two has no manifest, so it has no designs,

00:01:46.95 --> 00:01:51.97
and a manifest that can't be parsed is a five oh two that names the commit.

00:01:53.12 --> 00:01:58.05
A preview link carries a token: the project, the commit and an expiry,

00:01:58.05 --> 00:02:02.13
signed with an H-MAC under a key made when the API starts.

00:02:02.13 --> 00:02:07.89
One token covers the whole commit, so a mock's links to its own styles still work.

00:02:07.89 --> 00:02:13.24
It lasts sixty minutes, and a tampered or expired token is simply not found.

00:02:14.39 --> 00:02:18.79
The preview host is the API itself, but only on another host name.

00:02:18.79 --> 00:02:22.52
The route is mapped for the preview origin's host alone,

00:02:22.52 --> 00:02:25.59
so on Mission Control's host it doesn't exist.

00:02:25.59 --> 00:02:31.12
It serves only docs slash mocks and docs slash design system, at the signed commit,

00:02:31.12 --> 00:02:32.99
read from GitHub and cached.

00:02:32.99 --> 00:02:38.52
Every response says content security policy sandbox allow scripts, sends no cookie,

00:02:38.52 --> 00:02:41.59
and sets the theme on the page's html element.

00:02:42.74 --> 00:02:46.74
In the browser, the frame is sandboxed with allow scripts,

00:02:46.74 --> 00:02:48.67
and never allow same origin.

00:02:48.67 --> 00:02:51.98
So the page runs in an opaque origin of its own.

00:02:51.98 --> 00:02:57.42
Reading document dot cookie, local storage or the parent page throws a security

00:02:57.42 --> 00:02:57.83
error.

00:02:57.83 --> 00:03:03.55
And because cookies are kept per host, Mission Control's session never even reaches

00:03:03.55 --> 00:03:04.86
the preview origin.

00:03:06.01 --> 00:03:10.96
Width and theme are radio groups, with the arrow keys moving within each.

00:03:10.96 --> 00:03:14.35
The frame is laid out at exactly the chosen width,

00:03:14.35 --> 00:03:18.90
three seventy five up to twelve hundred pixels, then scaled to fit,

00:03:18.90 --> 00:03:22.35
so the mock's own media queries see the real width.

00:03:22.35 --> 00:03:26.15
The theme goes to the preview host as a query parameter.

00:03:27.30 --> 00:03:32.29
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found: there was no route.

00:03:32.29 --> 00:03:36.82
Now eight tests cover the listing, the sandbox headers and the theme,

00:03:36.82 --> 00:03:41.62
a preview pinned to its commit, the two allowed folders and nothing else,

00:03:41.62 --> 00:03:45.76
tampered and expired tokens and Mission Control's host refused,

00:03:45.76 --> 00:03:49.89
a project with no designs, an unreadable manifest, and Viewers.

00:03:50.24 --> 00:03:54.39
The isolation test runs a probe page inside the real frame.

00:03:54.39 --> 00:03:58.04
It reports its own origin as null, and that cookies,

00:03:58.04 --> 00:04:01.13
storage and the parent page are all blocked,

00:04:01.13 --> 00:04:04.71
while Mission Control itself has a readable cookie.

00:04:04.71 --> 00:04:09.69
Another test clicks X S to X L and watches the probe report each width,

00:04:09.69 --> 00:04:11.24
then switches to dark.

00:04:11.59 --> 00:04:15.56
All green, the backend has two hundred and twenty two tests,

00:04:15.56 --> 00:04:20.58
the worker one hundred and forty four, and the frontend five new view tests.

00:04:20.58 --> 00:04:24.62
End to end, there are fourteen new tests: five for behaviour,

00:04:24.62 --> 00:04:29.25
five visual comparisons with the mocks, and four accessibility checks.

00:04:30.40 --> 00:04:35.18
Here is Kiln Booking, after a real worker ran stages zero to two.

00:04:35.18 --> 00:04:40.26
Its Designs tab lists every screen and state that stage two exported,

00:04:40.26 --> 00:04:42.25
each with a live thumbnail.

00:04:42.25 --> 00:04:45.26
Choosing a state opens it in the preview.

00:04:45.26 --> 00:04:49.09
Then the sizes, from X S to X L, and dark, and back.

00:04:49.44 --> 00:04:52.49
At X S and dark, the frame is phone width.

00:04:52.49 --> 00:04:57.22
At X L it is laid out at twelve hundred pixels and scaled to fit.

00:04:57.22 --> 00:05:01.58
The tab works in Mission Control's dark mode and on a phone.

00:05:01.93 --> 00:05:06.31
Here is the probe in the real frame, served from another origin,

00:05:06.31 --> 00:05:07.96
with every read blocked.

00:05:07.96 --> 00:05:11.93
And a project before stage two says it has no designs yet,

00:05:11.93 --> 00:05:16.18
while an unreadable manifest names its commit, with try again.

00:05:17.33 --> 00:05:19.28
Journey fourteen is complete.

00:05:19.28 --> 00:05:24.88
The gallery lists every screen and state, with thumbnails and Magic Patterns links.

00:05:24.88 --> 00:05:29.26
Each preview comes from a separate origin, signed for one commit,

00:05:29.26 --> 00:05:32.36
in a sandbox that can't reach Mission Control.

00:05:32.36 --> 00:05:37.35
And the frame resizes from X S to X L and switches between light and dark.

00:05:37.35 --> 00:05:39.17
Next comes journey fifteen.
